    "# Design Justification and Schema Notes\n",
    "\n",
    "#### A. Why Above Design is Used ?\n",
    "\n",
    "The project schema provided in `Schema_part1.PDF` specifically instructs using **SSN as the primary key** for key entities such as `patients` and `physicians`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Project Note:**  \n",
    "*The provided schema is not the best ideal design, but just for simplicity and convenience. For example, generally you should not use SSN as primary key.*\n",
    "\n",
    "Despite this not being recommended in real-world database design, we adhered to the instructions to maintain consistency and meet the academic requirements.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### B. Why Using SSN as a Primary Key is Not Ideal ?\n",
    "\n",
    "In professional, production-level systems, using **Social Security Numbers (SSNs)** as primary keys is discouraged due to:\n",
    "\n",
    "-  **Privacy Concerns** — SSNs are personally identifiable information (PII) and sensitive in nature.\n",
    "-  **Legal and Ethical Risks** — Storing and indexing PII without proper protection violates compliance standards.\n",
    "-  **Lack of Flexibility** — SSNs may be changed or corrected; using them as keys can break referential integrity.\n",
    "-  **Scalability Issues** — Joining large tables on long string values like SSN is less performant than using integer keys.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### C. Why We Still Used SSN in This Project ?\n",
    "\n",
    "This academic project **requires** the use of SSNs as primary keys in the schema. Therefore:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Situation            | Action Taken                          |\n",
    "|----------------------|----------------------------------------|\n",
    "| Real-world design    | Use `AUTO_INCREMENT` or `UUID` surrogate keys; store SSNs securely as unique fields. |\n",
    "| Academic project     | Use SSN as primary key as per instructions in the schema. |\n",
    "\n",
    "#### D. Real-World Alternative Design (Production-Ready Schema)\n",
    "In a professional system, we would use surrogate keys (AUTO_INCREMENT or UUID) and keep SSNs as a secure, unique field.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### E. Key Benefits of This Approach\n",
    "\n",
    "| **Feature**              | **Benefit**                                                               |\n",
    "|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n",
    "| `AUTO_INCREMENT` IDs     | Ensures unique, system-generated identifiers for better scalability       |\n",
    "| `ssn` as UNIQUE          | Maintains uniqueness without exposing it as a primary key                 |\n",
    "| Referential Integrity    | More efficient joins using integer-based foreign keys                     |\n",
    "| Privacy & Compliance     | Easier to anonymize and protect sensitive information                     |\n",
    "\n",
    "#### F. Suggested Documentation Note\n",
    "\n",
    "**Note:**\n",
    "While using SSNs as primary keys is discouraged in real-world database design due to privacy, security, and data integrity concerns, this project follows the academic schema provided in Schema_part1.PDF. The use of SSN as a primary key was implemented strictly as instructed for learning purposes and is not recommended for production environments.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### G. Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "* The academic project schema required SSNs to be used as primary keys.\n",
    "* All foreign key references (like physician_id, patient_id) point to SSNs, as specified in the instructions.\n",
    "* A real-world production-grade schema would use system-generated IDs and treat SSNs as sensitive, unique data fields."
   ]
